WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.502
The RMF process is often described through seven steps: Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement,

00:00:05.502 --> 00:00:07.164
Assess, Authorize, and Monitor.

00:00:07.164 --> 00:00:11.319
These steps help organizations manage risk across the system lifecycle.

00:00:11.319 --> 00:00:14.228
Prepare establishes the organizational and system-level context.

00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:18.383
Teams identify stakeholders, missions, assumptions, common controls, risk management strategy,

00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:22.538
and the information needed to make later steps more effective.

00:00:22.538 --> 00:00:25.862
Categorize defines the potential impact if confidentiality, integrity,

00:00:25.862 --> 00:00:27.524
or availability are affected.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:33.757
Categorization shapes the level of rigor and helps determine which baseline controls may be appropriate.

00:00:33.757 --> 00:00:37.081
Select identifies the controls needed for the system.

00:00:37.081 --> 00:00:41.236
Implement turns those selected controls into real safeguards, configurations, procedures,

00:00:41.236 --> 00:00:43.314
architecture, automation, monitoring, and documentation.

00:00:43.314 --> 00:00:47.469
Assess evaluates whether controls are implemented correctly, operating as intended,

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:49.547
and producing the desired outcome.

00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:56.195
Authorize is the formal risk decision about whether the system may operate with the remaining risk.

00:00:56.195 --> 00:00:59.519
Monitor keeps the risk picture current after authorization.

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:04.090
Systems change, vulnerabilities appear, users change, dependencies shift, and threats evolve.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:08.660
Continuous monitoring helps the organization keep decisions grounded in current information.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:14.062
Although the steps are often shown in sequence, RMF is not purely linear.

00:01:14.062 --> 00:01:17.802
Findings may cause control changes, monitoring may trigger reassessment,

00:01:17.802 --> 00:01:21.957
and new mission needs may require updates to categorization, controls,

00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:23.203
or authorization conditions.

00:01:23.203 --> 00:01:26.528
That feedback loop is one of RMF's strengths.

00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:31.514
It lets organizations adjust the risk picture when new information appears instead

00:01:31.514 --> 00:01:36.500
of relying on assumptions from an earlier point in the system lifecycle.
